{"from": "site:antonia-fortress", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-18-03-roman-cohort", "grade": "B", "note": "The Herodian citadel stood 'in Jerusalem at the eastern end of the Second Wall, at the north-western corner of the Temple Mount' - the headquarters the Jerusalem-cohort claim presupposes."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-5-238-247", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-18-03-roman-cohort", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus: 'the temple was a fortress that guarded the city, as was the tower of Antonia a guard to the temple; and in that tower were the guards of those three' - a permanent Roman guard posted in the fortress adjoining the temple."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-5-238-247", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-18-12-chiliarchos", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus shows a permanent Roman guard stationed in the Antonia in Jerusalem, the force a chiliarchos would command; the passage does not name the rank, so grade C for the office's setting."}
{"from": "site:antonia-fortress", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-18-12-chiliarchos", "grade": "B", "note": "The excavated and model-attested citadel adjoining the Temple was the garrison post from which a commanding officer led troops."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-annas-gratus", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-18-13-annas-family", "grade": "B", "note": "Gratus the procurator 'deprived Ananus of the high priesthood' and the succession ran on under Roman appointment - Gratus next made Eleazar, the son of Ananus, high priest, and after two more one-year appointments 'Joseph Caiaphas was made his successor' - the family's grip on the office under Roman appointment, exactly John's setting. (Reviewer 9: the one-year span in the source is Eleazar's, not Ananus's; the former note read it as Ananus's.)"}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-198", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-18-13-annas-family", "grade": "B", "note": "The elder Ananus 'had five sons who had all performed the office of a high priest to God, and... had himself enjoyed that dignity a long time formerly' - the continuing family domination John presupposes."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-198", "type": "attests", "to": "person:annas-high-priest", "grade": "A", "note": "Names the elder Ananus who had long before held the high priesthood and whose sons all held it - the Annas of John 18:13 (the identification of Josephus's 'eldest Ananus' with Annas son of Seth is standard)."}
{"from": "text:talmud-sanhedrin-41a", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-18-31-capital-authority", "grade": "D", "note": "Reception text: 'forty years before the destruction of the [Second] Temple, capital punishment ceased in Israel' (traditionally c. 28 CE), because the Sanhedrin needed the procurator's approbation for death sentences. Redacted c. 500 CE, so it may not describe the 30s CE exactly."}
{"from": "text:yerushalmi-sanhedrin-1-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-18-31-capital-authority", "grade": "D", "note": "Reception text: 'Forty years before the Temple was destroyed, criminal jurisdiction was removed from Israel', with a modern footnote dating the removal to Judea's passage under direct Roman rule. Redacted c. 400 CE and later; it reports a rabbinic tradition, not a 30s CE record."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-199-sanhedrin", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-18-31-capital-authority", "grade": "B", "note": "In 62 CE 'it was not lawful for Ananus to assemble a sanhedrim without his consent' (the procurator Albinus's) - Judean authorities acted under Roman oversight in capital matters."}
{"from": "observation:jerusalem-april-night-cold", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-18-18-fire-cold", "grade": "C", "note": "April mean daily minimum 12.5 °C (1991-2020 normals): Passover-season nights in Jerusalem are cool enough for a courtyard fire; a modern climate normal, so context only."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-199-sanhedrin", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:jn-18-31-capital-authority", "note": "The same passage reports that Ananus assembled the Sanhedrin in 62 CE and had James stoned to death - the council did inflict a death sentence in this period, which a blanket 'not lawful to put anyone to death' must be reconciled with."}
{"from": "artifact:temple-warning-inscription", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:jn-18-31-capital-authority", "note": "The balustrade inscription warned pagan visitors 'not to proceed under penalty of death' - some capital jurisdiction existed in the Temple's own sphere, complicating a total absence of capital authority; who executed that penalty (Jewish or Roman authority) is debated."}
